- id: C:lemma/Nat.exists_prime_gt_modEq_one
  type: lemma
  name: Nat.exists_prime_gt_modEq_one
  summary: The theorem Nat.exists_prime_gt_modEq_one, as the Lean file declares it.
  stance: observed
  lean: Nat.exists_prime_gt_modEq_one
  form: theorem
  span:
    file: PrimesCongruentOne.lean
    lines: 28-59
  digest: b048a5d8e8afe80b
  says: For every natural number k and every natural number n, if k is not 0, then there is a natural number p such
    that p is prime, n < p, and p is congruent to 1 modulo k.
  reached: 'yes'
  depth: 2
  carries: 0
  proof: tactic
  sorry: 0
  automation:
  - simp
  used-by:
  - C:lemma/Nat.frequently_atTop_modEq_one
  made:
    move: survey
    run: 2026-11-22-1200-0001
    step: 1
    node: '1'
    engine: code
  role: spine
  method: For k = 1 it takes any prime above n; for k > 1 it takes the least prime factor p of the k-th cyclotomic
    polynomial evaluated at b = k times n factorial, so that b is a root of that polynomial modulo p, hence p does
    not divide b, hence p > n, and b has order exactly k modulo p, so k divides p - 1.
  why: Without it there is no source of primes congruent to 1 modulo k at all; it is the whole construction. It
    gives, for each bound n, one such prime beyond n, which turns into infinitely many with only a change of language.
  leans-on:
  - C:reliance/infinite-primes
  - C:reliance/cyclotomic-value-bound
  - C:reliance/least-factor-prime
  - C:reliance/cyclotomic-reduction
  - C:reliance/zero-mod-p
  - C:reliance/root-coprime
  - C:reliance/divides-factorial
  - C:reliance/fermat-order
  - C:reliance/cyclotomic-root-primitive
  - C:reliance/congruence-as-divisibility
  slack:
  - hypothesis: p is the least prime factor of the value
    would-suffice: p is any prime dividing the value
    lines: 41-48
    confidence: high
  provenance:
    mode: derived
    sources:
    - PrimesCongruentOne.lean#L28-L59
    confidence: 1
    state: known
