- id: P:yield/what-the-proof-delivers
  type: yield
  name: What the proof delivers
  summary: For each k other than 0 and each bound n the proof names one prime congruent to 1 modulo k above n.
  stance: opinion
  of: C:statement/Nat.infinite_setOfPred_prime_modEq_one
  beyond: 'yes'
  says: For each k other than 0 and each bound n the proof names one prime congruent to 1 modulo k above n. For
    k = 1 it is any prime at least n + 1. For k > 1 it is the least prime factor of the absolute value of the k-th
    cyclotomic polynomial at b = k times n factorial, and the argument uses only that p is a prime dividing that
    value, so every prime factor of that value is above n and congruent to 1 modulo k. The least one is at most
    the value itself. The statement keeps only that the set of such primes is infinite.
  as-statement: For every natural number k > 1 and every natural number n, every prime factor p of the absolute
    value of the k-th cyclotomic polynomial over the integers at k times n factorial satisfies n < p and p congruent
    to 1 modulo k; in particular there is such a prime p with n < p, and p at most that absolute value.
  needs:
  - Replace the least prime factor by an arbitrary prime factor; the steps at lines 43-59 use only that p is prime
    and divides the value.
  - Add that the least prime factor of a number greater than 1 is at most that number, for the upper end.
  bound: between
  chooses:
  - what: the point b at which the cyclotomic polynomial is evaluated
    where: C:lemma/Nat.exists_prime_gt_modEq_one
    value: k times n factorial
    gives: with b = k times m for any m at least 1 divisible by every number from 1 to n, each prime factor of the
      value is above n and congruent to 1 modulo k; the factor k keeps k nonzero modulo p, the factor m keeps p
      above n
    best: k times the least common multiple of 1 to n, which gives a smaller value and so a smaller upper end; the
      file takes n factorial
    confidence: medium
  - what: which prime factor of the value to take
    where: C:lemma/Nat.exists_prime_gt_modEq_one
    value: the least prime factor
    gives: any prime factor gives a prime above n congruent to 1 modulo k
    best: the file's choice, since the least factor gives the smallest witness
    confidence: high
  - what: the prime in the case k = 1
    where: C:lemma/Nat.exists_prime_gt_modEq_one
    value: a prime at least n + 1 from the infinitude of primes
    gives: a prime above n, trivially congruent to 1 modulo 1
    best: the file's choice
    confidence: high
  - what: the bound handed to the existence lemma
    where: C:lemma/Nat.frequently_atTop_modEq_one
    value: each n in turn
    gives: a prime p at least n with the property, the strict n < p weakened to n at most p
    best: the file's choice
    confidence: high
  trivial:
  - case: k = 1
    known: there are infinitely many primes, and every number is congruent to 1 modulo 1
    delivered: any prime at least n + 1, for each n
    compares: equal
  - case: k = 2
    known: there are infinitely many odd primes
    delivered: the prime factors of 2 times n factorial plus 1, each odd and above n
    compares: equal
  - case: k = 4
    known: prime factors of x squared plus 1 for even x are congruent to 1 modulo 4, so there are infinitely many
      such primes
    delivered: the prime factors of 16 times n factorial squared plus 1, each above n and congruent to 1 modulo
      4
    compares: equal
  grounds:
  - of: C:lemma/Nat.exists_prime_gt_modEq_one
    lines: 28-59
  - of: C:lemma/Nat.frequently_atTop_modEq_one
    lines: 61-65
  - of: C:statement/Nat.infinite_setOfPred_prime_modEq_one
    lines: 68-70
  confidence: high
  provenance:
    mode: derived
    sources:
    - PrimesCongruentOne.lean#L28-L59
    - PrimesCongruentOne.lean#L61-L65
    - PrimesCongruentOne.lean#L68-L70
    confidence: 0.5
    state: known
  made:
    move: construe
    run: 2026-11-22-1200-0004
    step: 1
    node: '1'
    engine: model:blind-decompiler
