- id: C:part/one-beyond-every-bound
  type: part
  name: One such prime beyond every bound
  summary: To show the set of primes congruent to 1 modulo k is infinite (k not 0), it is enough to produce, for
    each bound n, one such prime p with n < p; the later stages build that prime and the last stage turns it back
    into the infinite set.
  stance: opinion
  division: stage
  number: 1
  stage: reduction
  says: To show the set of primes congruent to 1 modulo k is infinite (k not 0), it is enough to produce, for each
    bound n, one such prime p with n < p; the later stages build that prime and the last stage turns it back into
    the infinite set.
  plain: The goal is to show there is no end to the whole numbers that only one and themselves divide and that leave
    a remainder of one when divided by a given fixed number. It is enough to show that beyond any number you name,
    one more such number can always be found.
  plain-if: none
  obvious: 'The obvious way copies the old proof that primes never run out: multiply the primes already found and
    add one. Its prime factors are new, but nothing forces them to be congruent to 1 modulo k, so this fails; the
    file instead evaluates the k-th cyclotomic polynomial, whose prime factors are forced into that class. The general
    theorem of Dirichlet would also give the result but is far heavier.'
  steps:
  - C:statement/Nat.infinite_setOfPred_prime_modEq_one
  regime:
    as: the bound n, any natural number
    uniform-in: every k other than 0
    fixed: none
    worst-over: none
  confidence: high
  provenance:
    mode: derived
    sources:
    - PrimesCongruentOne.lean#L68-L70
    confidence: 0.5
    state: known
  made:
    move: construe
    run: 2026-11-22-1200-0004
    step: 2
    node: '2'
    engine: model:blind-decompiler
- id: C:part/the-modulus-one
  type: part
  name: The modulus one
  summary: Since k is not 0, either k = 1 or k > 1.
  stance: opinion
  division: stage
  number: 2
  stage: side
  says: Since k is not 0, either k = 1 or k > 1. For k = 1 every number is congruent to 1, so any prime at least
    n + 1, from the infinitude of primes, serves; this clears the way for the cyclotomic stages, whose bound needs
    k > 1.
  plain: When the fixed number is one, the remainder condition asks nothing, so any whole number that only one and
    itself divide, lying beyond the named number, will do. The old fact that such numbers never run out supplies
    one.
  plain-if: none
  steps:
  - C:step/case-k-one
  confidence: high
  provenance:
    mode: derived
    sources:
    - PrimesCongruentOne.lean#L30-L32
    confidence: 0.5
    state: known
  made:
    move: construe
    run: 2026-11-22-1200-0004
    step: 2
    node: '2'
    engine: model:blind-decompiler
- id: C:part/the-candidate-prime
  type: part
  name: The candidate prime from the cyclotomic value
  summary: For k > 1 put b = k times n factorial, which is at least 2.
  stance: opinion
  division: stage
  number: 3
  stage: main
  says: For k > 1 put b = k times n factorial, which is at least 2. The absolute value of the k-th cyclotomic polynomial
    at b exceeds b - 1, so it exceeds 1 and its least prime factor p exists; because p divides that value, b is
    a root of the k-th cyclotomic polynomial modulo p. The next stages read off the two properties of p from this
    root.
  plain: For a fixed number above one, the proof builds a starting number by multiplying the fixed number by every
    whole number from one to the named number, then feeds it into a fixed formula made of its powers and finds that
    the outcome is larger than one. The smallest divisor of that outcome other than one is then a whole number that
    only one and itself divide, and the formula gives nothing over when the starting number is put in and everything
    is counted by remainders on division by that divisor.
  plain-if: the fixed number is greater than one, so that the outcome of the formula is greater than one
  steps:
  - C:lemma/Nat.exists_prime_gt_modEq_one
  - C:step/value-exceeds-one
  - C:step/least-prime-factor
  - C:step/root-mod-p
  regime:
    as: exact, for each k > 1 and each n
    uniform-in: n
