- id: C:part/past-the-bound
  type: part
  name: The candidate lies past the bound
  summary: A root of a cyclotomic polynomial modulo p is coprime to p, so p does not divide b.
  stance: opinion
  division: stage
  number: 4
  stage: main
  says: A root of a cyclotomic polynomial modulo p is coprime to p, so p does not divide b. Every number from 1
    to n divides n factorial and so divides b; a prime p at most n would therefore divide b, which it does not,
    so n < p.
  plain: Because of how the formula is built, the divisor found cannot also divide the starting number. Every whole
    number from one to the named number does divide the starting number, so the divisor is none of them and lies
    beyond the named number.
  plain-if: the starting number is a multiple of every whole number from one to the named number
  steps:
  - C:step/p-not-dividing-b
  - C:step/p-above-n
  regime:
    as: exact, for each n
    uniform-in: n
    fixed: k
    worst-over: none
  confidence: high
  provenance:
    mode: derived
    sources:
    - PrimesCongruentOne.lean#L49-L50
    - PrimesCongruentOne.lean#L51-L52
    confidence: 0.5
    state: known
  made:
    move: construe
    run: 2026-11-22-1200-0004
    step: 2
    node: '2'
    engine: model:blind-decompiler
- id: C:part/order-exactly-k
  type: part
  name: The order of b is exactly k
  summary: Since b is nonzero modulo p, its multiplicative order divides p - 1.
  stance: opinion
  division: stage
  number: 5
  stage: main
  says: Since b is nonzero modulo p, its multiplicative order divides p - 1. Since k divides b and p does not divide
    b, k is nonzero modulo p, so the root b is a primitive k-th root of unity and its order is exactly k. Hence
    k divides p - 1, so p is congruent to 1 modulo k, which finishes the existence lemma.
  plain: Multiplying the starting number by itself again and again, keeping only the remainder on division by the
    divisor, returns to one for the first time after exactly the fixed number of rounds. Such a cycle always fits
    a whole number of times into one less than the divisor, so the divisor leaves a remainder of one when divided
    by the fixed number.
  plain-if: the divisor does not divide the fixed number, which holds because the fixed number divides the starting
    number and the divisor does not
  steps:
  - C:step/order-is-k
  confidence: high
  provenance:
    mode: derived
    sources:
    - PrimesCongruentOne.lean#L53-L59
    confidence: 0.5
    state: known
  made:
    move: construe
    run: 2026-11-22-1200-0004
    step: 2
    node: '2'
    engine: model:blind-decompiler
- id: C:part/back-to-infinitely-many
  type: part
  name: Back to infinitely many
  summary: For every n the existence lemma gives a prime p with n < p, so with n at most p; on the natural numbers
    this is the same as the property holding frequently at infinity, which is the same as the set where it holds
    being infinite.
  stance: opinion
  division: stage
  number: 6
  stage: close
  says: For every n the existence lemma gives a prime p with n < p, so with n at most p; on the natural numbers
    this is the same as the property holding frequently at infinity, which is the same as the set where it holds
    being infinite. This is the statement.
  plain: Having one such number beyond every named number is restated as meeting them again and again with no last
    one, and that is restated once more as there being endlessly many of them.
  plain-if: none
  steps:
  - C:lemma/Nat.frequently_atTop_modEq_one
  confidence: high
